※ pl4g-test: compile=fail
※ pl4g-test: expect-diag 4241 LANG_NUMBER_NOT_A_FLOAT
※ pl4g-test: expect-diag 4241 LANG_NUMBER_NOT_A_FLOAT
※ pl4g-test: expect-diag 4241 LANG_NUMBER_NOT_A_FLOAT
※ pl4g-test: expect-diag 4240 LANG_NUMBER_NOT_AN_INTEGER

※ `⎕trunc` and `⎕bits` take a floating-point number, and `⎕from_bits` makes one.
※ A whole number truncated toward zero is itself and its bits are already a
※ whole number, so neither has anything to do with one; and what `⎕trunc`
※ makes is a whole number, which a floating-point type is not.

let std := ⎕import("std")

let n: u32 = 7u32
let x: f64 = 2.5f64

@[startup]
fn main(init: std.Init) → u6:
    let a: u8 = ⎕trunc(n, ⌜u8⌝) ?? 0u8
    let b: u32 = ⎕bits(n)
    let c: u32 = ⎕from_bits(n, ⌜u32⌝)
    let d: f32 = ⎕trunc(x, ⌜f32⌝) ?? 0.0f32
    0u6
